      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Imperative and reactive transaction management share the same semantics for transaction boundary and transaction attribute definitions. The main difference between imperative and reactive transactions is the deferred nature of the latter. "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "TransactionInterceptor"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " decorates the returned reactive type with a transactional operator to begin and clean up the transaction. Therefore, calling a transactional reactive method defers the actual transaction management to a subscription type that activates processing of the reactive type."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Another aspect of reactive transaction management relates to data escaping which is a natural consequence of the programming model."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Method return values of imperative transactions are returned from transactional methods upon successful termination of a method so that partially computed results do not escape the method closure."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Reactive transaction methods return a reactive wrapper type which represents a computation sequence along with a promise to begin and complete the computation."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "A "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "Publisher"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " can emit data while a transaction is ongoing but not necessarily completed. Therefore, methods that depend upon successful completion of an entire transaction need to ensure completion and buffer results in the calling code."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      }
    ],
    "outline": null,
